WEBVTT

00:00.770 --> 00:03.720
Let's take the
derivative with respect

00:03.720 --> 00:08.570
to x of x to the n
plus 1-th power over n

00:08.570 --> 00:12.460
plus 1 plus some constant c.

00:12.460 --> 00:14.210
And we're going to
assume here, because we

00:14.210 --> 00:16.420
want this expression
to be defined,

00:16.420 --> 00:20.490
we're going to assume that
n does not equal negative 1.

00:20.490 --> 00:22.740
If it equaled negative
1, we'd be dividing by 0,

00:22.740 --> 00:24.724
and we haven't defined
what that means.

00:24.724 --> 00:26.140
So let's take the
derivative here.

00:26.140 --> 00:29.500
So this is going to be equal
to-- well, the derivative of x

00:29.500 --> 00:31.800
to the n plus 1
over n plus 1, we

00:31.800 --> 00:34.120
can just use the
power rule over here.

00:34.120 --> 00:36.580
So our exponent is n plus 1.

00:36.580 --> 00:38.360
We can bring it out front.

00:38.360 --> 00:45.870
So it's going to be n
plus 1 times x to the-- I

00:45.870 --> 00:48.160
want to use that same color.

00:48.160 --> 00:52.260
Colors are the hard part--
times x to the-- instead of n

00:52.260 --> 00:53.944
plus 1, we subtract
1 from the exponent.

00:53.944 --> 00:55.110
This is just the power rule.

00:55.110 --> 00:58.790
So n plus 1 minus
1 is going to be n.

00:58.790 --> 01:01.970
And then we can't forget that we
were dividing by this n plus 1.

01:01.970 --> 01:04.629
So we have divided by n plus 1.

01:04.629 --> 01:06.600
And then we have plus c.

01:06.600 --> 01:09.300
The derivative of a constant
with respect to x-- a constant

01:09.300 --> 01:12.050
does not change as x
changes, so it is just

01:12.050 --> 01:15.250
going to be 0, so plus 0.

01:15.250 --> 01:17.740
And since n is not
equal to negative 1,

01:17.740 --> 01:19.860
we know that this is
going to be defined.

01:19.860 --> 01:21.630
This is just going to
be something divided

01:21.630 --> 01:23.810
by itself, which is
just going to be 1.

01:23.810 --> 01:28.420
And this whole thing
simplifies to x to the n.

01:28.420 --> 01:30.170
So the derivative of
this thing-- and this

01:30.170 --> 01:33.140
is a very general terms--
is equal to x to the n.

01:33.140 --> 01:37.390
So given that, what is
the antiderivative-- let

01:37.390 --> 01:39.050
me switch colors here.

01:39.050 --> 01:48.230
What is the antiderivative
of x to the n?

01:48.230 --> 01:50.090
And remember, this
is just the kind

01:50.090 --> 01:51.619
of strange-looking
notation we use.

01:51.619 --> 01:54.160
It'll make more sense when we
start doing definite integrals.

01:54.160 --> 01:57.120
But what is the
antiderivative of x to the n?

01:57.120 --> 01:59.120
And we could say the
antiderivative with respect

01:59.120 --> 02:00.590
to x, if we want to.

02:00.590 --> 02:03.255
And another way of calling this
is the indefinite integral.

02:07.840 --> 02:10.090
Well, we know this
is saying x to the n

02:10.090 --> 02:11.752
is the derivative of what?

02:11.752 --> 02:12.960
Well, we just figured it out.

02:12.960 --> 02:14.120
It's the derivative
of this thing,

02:14.120 --> 02:15.911
and we've written it
in very general terms.

02:15.911 --> 02:19.580
We're actually encapsulating
multiple constants here.

02:19.580 --> 02:21.670
We could have x to
the n plus 1 over n

02:21.670 --> 02:25.300
plus 1 plus 0, plus 1, plus
2, plus pi, plus a billion.

02:25.300 --> 02:31.140
So this is going to be equal
to x to the n plus 1 over n

02:31.140 --> 02:34.110
plus 1 plus c.

02:34.110 --> 02:35.920
So this is pretty powerful.

02:35.920 --> 02:40.920
You can kind of view this
as the reverse power rule.

02:40.920 --> 02:44.560
And it applies
for any n, as long

02:44.560 --> 02:46.500
as n does not equal negative 1.

02:46.500 --> 02:47.750
Let me make that very clear.

02:47.750 --> 02:52.590
n does not equal negative 1.

02:52.590 --> 02:54.310
Once again, this thing
would be undefined

02:54.310 --> 02:57.120
if n were equal to negative 1.

02:57.120 --> 02:58.720
So let's do a couple
of examples just

02:58.720 --> 03:01.050
to apply this-- you could
call it the reverse power

03:01.050 --> 03:04.330
rule if you want, or
the anti-power rule.

03:04.330 --> 03:10.730
So let's take the antiderivative
of x to the fifth power.

03:10.730 --> 03:13.574
What is the antiderivative
of x to the fifth?

03:13.574 --> 03:15.240
Well, all we have to
say is, well, look,

03:15.240 --> 03:16.950
the 5 is equal to the n.

03:16.950 --> 03:19.610
We just have to increment
the exponent by 1.

03:19.610 --> 03:24.280
So this is going to be equal
to x to the 5 plus 1 power.

03:24.280 --> 03:26.772
And then we divide
by that same value.

03:26.772 --> 03:28.980
Whatever the exponent was
when you increment it by 1,

03:28.980 --> 03:32.229
we divide by that same
value, divided by 5 plus 1.

03:32.229 --> 03:33.770
And of course, we
want to encapsulate

03:33.770 --> 03:35.810
all of the possible
antiderivatives,

03:35.810 --> 03:37.770
so you put the c
right over there.

03:37.770 --> 03:43.380
So this is going to be equal to
x to the sixth over 6 plus c.

03:43.380 --> 03:44.210
And you can verify.

03:44.210 --> 03:46.370
Take the derivative of
this using the power rule,

03:46.370 --> 03:49.830
you indeed get x to the fifth.

03:49.830 --> 03:51.560
Let's try another one.

03:51.560 --> 03:54.010
Let's try-- now
we'll do it in blue.

03:54.010 --> 03:58.540
Let's try the antiderivative
of-- let's make it interesting.

03:58.540 --> 04:06.290
Let's make it 5 times x to
the negative 2 power dx.

04:06.290 --> 04:08.710
So how would we evaluate this?

04:08.710 --> 04:10.290
Well, one simplification
you can do--

04:10.290 --> 04:14.420
and I haven't rigorously
proven it to you just yet--

04:14.420 --> 04:16.180
but we know that scalars
can go in and out

04:16.180 --> 04:18.804
of the derivative operator when
you're multiplying by a scalar.

04:18.804 --> 04:23.140
So this is, indeed, equal to
5 times the antiderivative

04:23.140 --> 04:26.605
of x to the negative
2 power, dx.

04:26.605 --> 04:28.710
And now we can just use,
I guess we could call it

04:28.710 --> 04:30.960
this anti-power rule,
so this is going

04:30.960 --> 04:38.840
to be equal to 5 times x to
the negative 2 power plus 1

04:38.840 --> 04:45.310
over the negative 2 power plus
1 plus some constant right

04:45.310 --> 04:47.320
over here.

04:47.320 --> 04:52.010
And then we can rewrite this as
5 times negative 2 power plus 1

04:52.010 --> 04:56.340
is x to the negative 1
over negative 2 plus 1

04:56.340 --> 04:59.330
is negative 1,
plus some constant.

04:59.330 --> 05:04.570
And this is equal to 5 times
negative x to the negative 1

05:04.570 --> 05:06.540
plus some constant.

05:06.540 --> 05:09.110
And then if we want, we
can distribute the 5.

05:09.110 --> 05:12.820
So this is equal to negative
5x to the negative 1.

05:12.820 --> 05:16.340
Now, we could write plus
5 times some constant,

05:16.340 --> 05:18.460
but this is just an
arbitrary constant.

05:18.460 --> 05:21.102
So this is still just
an arbitrary constant.

05:21.102 --> 05:22.560
So maybe we could
[INAUDIBLE] this.

05:22.560 --> 05:24.809
If you want it to show that
it's a different constant,

05:24.809 --> 05:27.370
you could say this
is c1, c1, c1.

05:27.370 --> 05:30.210
You multiply 5 times c1,
you get another constant.

05:30.210 --> 05:34.304
We could just call that c,
which is equal to 5 times c1.

05:34.304 --> 05:35.220
But there you have it.

05:35.220 --> 05:39.640
Negative 5x to the
negative 1 plus c.

05:39.640 --> 05:42.190
And once again, all of these,
try to evaluate the derivative,

05:42.190 --> 05:46.550
and you will see that you
get this business, right over

05:46.550 --> 05:48.100
there.
